  "articleBody": "Rotted or damaged trim requires more than a quick cosmetic patch. Rushing through repairs without fixing underlying problems leads to recurring damage. Here are frequent mistakes homeowners face when addressing damaged woodwork.\n\n1. Caulking over decayed wood. Applying caulk or filler over soft, rotted wood traps moisture inside and lets deterioration continue hidden behind the new paint.\n\n2. Replacing trim without fixing the water leak. If window moisture or foundation runoff caused the initial rot, new trim will quickly rot as well. Always find and fix the water source first.\n\n3. Skipping essential drying time. When dealing with exterior moisture, rushing to install or paint replacement pieces before the substrate dries (often needing 24 to 48 hours) locks moisture into wall cavities.\n\n4. Mismatching decorative profiles. Installing modern stock directly against historic baseboards or door casings creates awkward, glaring joints that ruin room aesthetics.\n\n5. Omitting primer on cut ends. Leaving fresh cuts or end grain unsealed allows exterior moisture to wick directly into replacement boards, causing early swelling and paint failure.\n\nWhat to Do Next\nIf damage reaches underlying wall cavities or involves exterior window flashing, professional repair ensures lasting protection. HH Remodeling LLC delivers expert finish carpentry and restoration services in Aurora, Centennial, and across Denver. Call 303-505-0866 to discuss your trim project.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
